A||||7||NOCONES||What are the weak areas of the 2.5L??||||1||
Z||000000||NOCONES||12-27-2001||06:12 PM||||I have the engine out of an 89 S-10.  I know that overreving this engine is a major no no.  but i would like to do a performance engine rebuild, and need to know where to concentrate my efforts.   would a heavy duty bottom end be a good start.  What comonly fails under high.. non overreving abuse.  also how high can the engine rev reliably.  Any info would be appreciated.<P>oh.. and i already know the hole engine is the weak part yadda yadda.. but thanks anyway||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||12-27-2001||07:46 PM||||worse part would be between the rear wheels!!!!!! j/k  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> sorry just had to say that, no flame intended.||||reg||10
Z||000002||BlackStang95||12-27-2001||08:33 PM||||Im with you roger, I was gonna say the horsepower output!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Seriously your efforts are prolly better focused in a 2.8 or 3.4 swap.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Posthumane||12-28-2001||12:05 AM||||Well the cast iron block isn't too great for one. If you wanted a performace duke, your best bet would be the Super duty (but expensive!). It's got restrictive heads, which you can easily fix with a dremel tool (i can't remember the details, just do a search). I think a heavy duty bottom end would increase the allowable revs a bit. Basically if you want a hi-perf reliable duke, you have to change everything that has a chance of failing under stress - crank, pistons, rods, valve springs (to prevent float), port the heads, get a better cam, stronger head to block bolts (maybe more of them if you can)... umm dunno what else. Get a less restrictive intake/exhaust (maybe no cat?). That's all I can think of. Maybe a light pressure turbo boost, but you have to be careful with this one because of the previously mentioned weak block.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||12-28-2001||12:19 PM||||I read somewhere that the S-10 is the strongest Duke block. The 88 crank is supposedly the strongest Duke crank that was installed in a Fiero. This is due to the extra metal used to drive the force balancer in the oil pan. <BR>I was going to post links, but the sites are "temporarily inaccessible".<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||ITB 2M4||12-28-2001||07:28 PM||||Are you going to use that block in your Fiero?  Cause the starter mount for the rear wheel drive 2.5's are on the wrong side.<P>You should be able to find some suitable small block piston's and rods, or if you want to use stock stuff, take it all (flywheel, crank, rods, pistons) down to a balancing shop and have the stuff 'balanced and blueprinted'.  I'm using stock stuff in my 2.5, with ARP rod bolts for a 283ci Chevy.  They are a little bit longer than the stock ones, but a hell of a lot stronger.  Make sure you mark each rod as to which hole it came out of (a little mark with a center punch, 1 punch for the number 1 rod, 2 punches for the number 2 rod....), that way they when you put it together, you have the piston and rod assembly that were balanced together assembled together.  Getting the flywheel lightened and balanced will probably do wonders for the life of the crank, but your 'flywheel effect' may suffer a little (leaving stoplights, you may bog a little bit at low revs).  If you get a remaned crank, or turn your old one, I wouldn't cut it down more than .010, anything more will just make the crank weaker.<P>With my 'stock' 2.5, I'm revving over 5k rpm all the time, with no problems.  If my class allowed more cam, I could probably go higher, but it's not making any power beyond 5200rpm right now.<BR><P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-28-2001||07:54 PM||||GM started consolidating all the cast iron 4 cylinder blocks.... that I'm aware of they got it down to one with and one without the balancer unit for FWD/Fiero. <P>The last S10 I looked at appeared to be drilled for a starter on both sides but I couldn't tell for sure. (Was too sick to crawl under it at the time.) The other thing I'm not sure of is if the S10 block is set up to accept the Fiero/FWD waterpump which bolts to the side of the block.<P>The truck and FWD blocks both ran DIS from 87 on but the truck uses an EGR solinoid like the Fiero V6.<P>All the later blocks should be stronger than the pre DIS ones, but how much I couldn't say.<P>Also, I don't think you can put a balancer motor crank into a non balancer setup.<P>I doubt you'll get any combination that will rev really high. ballance and blue print would be a requirement. not an option. I'd replace both the rods and pistons. The OE ones aren't made for allot of abuse. The truck ones may be a little stronger but I doubt much. S10's using that block were built to be cheap. not fast. The Duke is more a torque monster than a high spinner. It makes more torque for it's size than allot of other motors. (Ignoring turbo/super...)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||NOCONES||12-28-2001||08:15 PM||||wow, i'm impressed, only two yank it out coments.<BR>i'm not sure how useful this information has become, as i've been looking at what it would take to build the engine i was thinking about, and i'm thinking the money would be better spent on a 3800, caddy 4.9, or 350 swap.  <BR>my origional intent was build the botom end, find a superduty head, port it, build custom intake, with multiport fuel injection, then turbo it, hoping with the right botom end i would get 275-300 reliable horsepower.  I know it's possible, but some checking has neted a price tag of the near 6K mark, for the engine alone.. let alone the addition of 3K motec engine managment..  but thanks for all the info. maybe at a later date i will try the built 2.5L but for now i think i might just go with a good ol swap||||reg||1
